  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nLingering rain and embedded thunderstorms will continue to\ndissipate and move south behind a departing upper wave this\nmorning. High pressure and slightly drier air will attempt to\nwork in from the northeast this afternoon partially clearing out\nmuch of the region although heating and abundant ground based\nmoisture will allow for destructive cumulus. Another impulse in\nthe persistent northwesterly flow is likely to trigger\nadditional showers and thunderstorms late this afternoon and\ntonight mainly along the western Finger Lakes, Southern Tier and\nNortheast Pennsylvania. Severe parameters set up best to the\nsouthwest of the CWA but can't completely rule strong storms out\nwith a SPC Marginal clipping our area. \n\nFriday should turn out the quietest day of this week however\nmodel guidance still suggests another upper wave skirting the\narea over WNY and north central PA during the afternoon and\nevening. Will therefore still have to carry some chance of\nshowers and thunderstorms again along the Southern Tier and\nportions of NEPA mainly west of I-81. Best instability and \nshear look to be south of the region tomorrow so severe storms \nare not expected.\n\nTemperatures will be a little tricky again today with morning \nclouds holding on, but then any sun breaking free this afternoon\nshould cause quick rebounds. I'll stick with the blended NBM \nfor now with upper 70s-lower 80s expected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n  \nA closed upper low will drop south into the Maritimes this\nweekend with a broad area of surface high pressure building down\nfrom Canada. This pattern will be favorable for drier conditions\nand near seasonal temperatures in the 70s during the day and \n50s/low 60s at night. This quiet weather should last through \nMonday with a slow rebound of warmer, more humid conditions \nduring the middle of next week as high pressure settles over the\nsoutheastern States and re-establishes southerly flow into the \nregion. Next better chance for showers and thunderstorms will be\nduring this midweek time frame given the increasing instability\ninteracting with passing upper waves.",
